\section{Higher-order goal%
  \BvrHint{\ldots secondary framing}}
This project aims to make essential medicine more affordable and
safer to purchase for Indian patients. While the topic is broader
than any single feature, the immediate engineering objective is to
translate \emph{price fairness and purchase safety} into a
measurable, deployable software solution: exposing price
manipulation relative to government-regulated ceilings, and
surfacing dangerous drug interactions before purchase.

\section{Time-to-value %
\BvrHint{\ldots primary heuristic}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.7cm]
    \item We will deliver meaningful increments quickly by prototyping the core prescription-to-comparison workflow and validating it against real prescriptions and real pharmacy pricing data within a short iteration window.
    \item We will prioritize fast feedback over perfection by using weekly iterations with continuous integration from the first iteration, so that feedback on OCR accuracy, price-comparison correctness, and anomaly-detection quality is available early and used to steer subsequent work.
    \item Success is defined by what can be delivered and measured in the first iteration, then improved based on user feedback.
\end{itemize}

\section{Problem Statement}
Medicine price comparison and drug-safety checking in India currently suffer from two distinct gaps, confirmed by a review of eight price-comparison platforms and four drug-interaction checkers:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.7cm]
    \item \textbf{Unverified price anomalies:} existing Indian price-comparison platforms display scraped prices without checking deviation from the NPPA government ceiling price, and none explain why a price looks anomalous.
    \item \textbf{Disconnected safety checking:} drug-interaction checking exists only as a separate US-market product category, never combined with Indian price comparison, generic-substitute trust scoring, or OCR-to-comparison in one pipeline.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Why this matters now (contextual relevance):} a price-comparison tool is only as trustworthy as its ability to flag manipulated prices, and a medicine-purchase workflow is only as safe as its ability to warn against dangerous combinations before purchase --- which motivates addressing both as first-class engineering concerns rather than as separate, disconnected features.

