\subsection{Data scraping platform matrix %
\BvrHint{\ldots platform-specific routing}}
Each of the five target pharmacy platforms renders pricing differently. \texttt{fetch-service} routes each platform to the cheaper viable scraping method via a platform router (FR-12), avoiding a one-size-fits-all approach that would either waste compute (headless browser everywhere) or fail outright (static scraper on JS-rendered pages).

{\small
\begin{longtable}{@{}p{2.3cm}p{2.6cm}p{4.0cm}p{5.3cm}@{}}
\caption{Data Scraping Platform Matrix} \\
\toprule
\textbf{Platform} & \textbf{Rendering} & \textbf{Method} & \textbf{Notes} \\
\midrule
\endhead
1mg & Server-rendered & Static scraper (Cheerio\slash{}Scrapy) & Pricing present in initial HTML response \\
PharmEasy & Client-side JS & Headless browser (Playwright) & Pricing loads asynchronously after mount \\
Netmeds & Server-rendered & Static scraper & Similar to 1mg \\
Truemeds & Client-side JS & Headless browser & Requires network-idle wait before price is readable \\
Apollo 24\slash{}7 & Mixed & Headless browser (fallback from static) & Static attempted first; falls back to Playwright \\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}
}

Anti-bot resilience (FR-13, NFR-6) relies on randomized request delays, header spoofing, Playwright stealth flags, asset-load interception, and per-platform circuit breaking. On failure, the system falls back to cached data, then seed\slash{}fixture data, rather than omitting a platform from the comparison table (FR-15, NFR-5).

