\section{Functional \& Non-Functional Requirements %
\BvrHint{\ldots traceable and testable}}
The following requirements are traceable to specific services, database tables, or feature folders in the Medkart.ai repository.

\subsection{Functional requirements (selected)}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.7cm]
    \item \textbf{User \& Authentication:} FR-1: Register\slash{}login via email or phone and password. FR-2: Issue and validate JWT tokens. FR-3: View and update profile information.
    \item \textbf{Prescription Input \& OCR:} FR-4\slash{}5: Upload a prescription photo and extract drug\slash{}dosage\slash{}frequency via OCR. FR-6: Search directly, bypassing OCR. FR-7: LLM structures raw OCR text.
    \item \textbf{Medicine Normalization:} FR-8: Fuzzy-match names to canonical catalog. FR-9: Resolve brand to salt composition. FR-10: Log normalization decisions with match score.
    \item \textbf{Multi-Platform Price Fetching:} FR-11: Fetch live prices from all 5 platforms. FR-12: Route to static\slash{}headless scraper. FR-13: Apply anti-bot measures. FR-14\slash{}15: Cache-check and fallback. FR-16: Unified price schema.
    \item \textbf{AI Enrichment:} FR-17: Isolation Forest anomaly flag vs.\ NPPA. FR-18: SHAP explanations. FR-19: Generic substitute ranking. FR-20: LLM-based interaction warnings. FR-21: Safety guardrail on all LLM output.
    \item \textbf{Ranking, Real-Time Delivery \& Alerts:} FR-22\slash{}23: Weighted ranking, side-by-side display. FR-24: Progressive WebSocket\slash{}SSE streaming. FR-25\slash{}26: Price history and prescription retention. FR-27\slash{}28: Watchlist and target-price alerts.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Non-functional requirements (selected)}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.7cm]
    \item \textbf{Performance:} cached results under 2s (NFR-1); full live comparison under 15s with streaming (NFR-2).
    \item \textbf{Scalability:} modular monolith, independently scalable service boundaries (NFR-3); horizontally scalable fetch layer (NFR-4).
    \item \textbf{Reliability:} graceful degradation to cache\slash{}seed data (NFR-5); anti-bot resilience over retry-only (NFR-6).
    \item \textbf{Security:} hashed passwords (NFR-7); JWT on all non-auth endpoints (NFR-8).
    \item \textbf{Maintainability:} centralized shared packages (NFR-9); independently deployable services (NFR-10).
    \item \textbf{Data accuracy:} confidence-thresholded matching (NFR-11); TimescaleDB hypertables (NFR-12).
    \item \textbf{Usability:} single dashboard for 5 platforms (NFR-13); plain-language explanation for every AI flag (NFR-14).
    \item \textbf{Portability:} Docker Compose (NFR-15).
    \item \textbf{Observability:} centralized logs\slash{}traces\slash{}metrics (NFR-16); automated test suites across e2e\slash{}integration\slash{}contract\slash{}performance\slash{}security (NFR-17).
\end{itemize}

\section{Evaluation Criterion %
\BvrHint{\ldots measurable and attributable}}
We define success using measurable metrics tied to the core pipeline.

\subsection{Primary evaluation metric}
\textbf{F1 score of the Isolation Forest anomaly detector} vs.\ a rule-based fixed-threshold baseline, measured on a labelled NPPA-referenced dataset.
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.7cm]
    \item Attribution: measured via comparison against ground-truth labels on held-out listings.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Secondary evaluation metrics}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.7cm]
    \item \textbf{Anomaly detection:} precision, recall, false-positive rate, enrichment latency.
    \item \textbf{Interaction checking:} precision\slash{}recall vs.\ keyword-match baseline; guardrail pass rate.
    \item \textbf{OCR\slash{}normalization:} field-level OCR accuracy; match-confidence distribution.
    \item \textbf{Comparison performance:} cached response $<$2s (NFR-1); full comparison $<$15s (NFR-2).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Pilot validation plan %
\BvrHint{\ldots high-level}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.7cm]
    \item Recruit 40--60 student\slash{}staff volunteers for a 2--3 week campus pilot.
    \item Track anomaly-flag rate, interaction-flag rate, OCR correction rate, and repeat usage.
\end{itemize}

\section{Scalability %
\BvrHint{\ldots with foresight}}
The proposition should scale in both theoretical and practical senses.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=0.7cm]
    \item \textbf{Scalable (architecturally):} The system should support growth in prescriptions processed and concurrent comparisons by:
    \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.7cm]
        \item organizing the backend as a modular monolith with independently extractable service boundaries (NFR-3),
        \item keeping the fetch layer horizontally scalable (NFR-4),
        \item using a stateless REST API behind a single gateway.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Operationally deployable (practically):} The system should run on common managed infrastructure:
    \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.7cm]
        \item managed PostgreSQL\slash{}TimescaleDB,
        \item Docker Compose for local development,
        \item a CI\slash{}CD pipeline (already scaffolded) for staging\slash{}production releases.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\section{Engine availability heuristic}
A mature set of ``engines'' (tooling\slash{}services) is available to implement this as a web-based project:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.7cm]
    \item React for the web frontend, with React Native available for a future mobile client.
    \item Node.js\slash{}TypeScript microservices behind a REST API gateway with JWT authentication.
    \item Tesseract\slash{}Veryfi for OCR, RapidFuzz for fuzzy matching, and scikit-learn's Isolation Forest with SHAP for explainable anomaly detection.
    \item Managed PostgreSQL with the TimescaleDB extension, plus Redis for caching.
    \item Puppeteer\slash{}Playwright and Cheerio\slash{}Scrapy for hybrid, platform-aware scraping.
    \item GitHub Actions for CI\slash{}CD and Docker for containerized, portable deployment on AWS.
\end{itemize}
We will use these mature components to focus on pipeline correctness, safety guardrails, and measurement rather than inventing new infrastructure.

